To generate an automatic table of contents (ToC) in a LaTeX document, you can use the `tocpackage` and its associated commands. Follow these steps:

1. Include the `tocpackage` in your LaTeX document:
   Add the following line at the beginning of your LaTeX document, before `\begin{document}`:
   ```
   \usepackage{tocloft}
   ```
   This package provides additional control over the appearance and layout of the table of contents.

2. Create a ToC using `\tableofcontents`:
   Before the sections and chapters of your document, insert the command `\tableofcontents`. This command generates the ToC based on the information provided in your document.

3. Customizing ToC appearance:
   Depending on your document's style and needs, you may want to make some adjustments to the ToC appearance using `tocloft` commands. Here are some examples:

   a. Changing the ToC title:
      To customize the ToC title, use the command `\renewcommand{\contentsname}{<New ToC Title>}`. For example, to change the standard "Contents" title to "Section Overview", you would include the following command:
      ```
      \renewcommand{\contentsname}{Section Overview}
      ```

   b. Modifying ToC font size:
      To change the font size of the ToC, you can use the command `\fontsize{<size>}{<baselineskip>}:
      ```
      \renewcommand{\cfttoctitlefont}{\hfill\fontsize{14}{16}\selectfont}
      ```

   c. Adjusting ToC indentation:
      You can adjust the indentation of ToC entries using the command `\cftsetindents{<entries>}{<indent>}{<numindent>}`, where `<entries>` is the type of ToC entry (e.g., `chapter`, `section`, `subsection`), `<indent>` is the indent value, and `<numindent>` is the number of indents. For example, to indent all entries by one inch (2.54 cm), you could use:
      ```
      \cftsetindents{chapter,section,subsection}{2.54cm}{0pt}
      ```

4. Partial or selective ToC:
   If you want to create a ToC for only specific sections or chapters, you can use the `\addcontentsline{<file>}{<level>}{<title>}` command, where `<file>` is the file type ("toc" for table of contents), `<level>` is the section level (chapter, section, subsection, etc.), and `<title>` is the title of the section you want to add to the ToC. For example, to add only chapter 1 to the ToC:
